    "text": "Recall that the standard greedy algorithm for the set cover problem returns a(lnn+1)-approximation. We show that our algo- rithm implements such a greedy algorithm and returns a (lnn+1)- approximation in the set cover instance (U\u2032,S\u2032). At the beginning of an iteration i, without loss of generality, assume that our algo- rithm has selected the pairs (SA1,SB1),...,(SAi\u22121,SBi\u22121), so the sets SA1,B1,...,SAi\u22121,Bi\u22121have been selected for the set cover instance(U\u2032,S\u2032). LetU\u2212be the set of uncovered elements in U\u2032. Let SAj,Bjbe the set inS\u2032that covers the maximum number of elements in U\u2212. We consider two cases. IfSAj\u2208\u0338{SA1,...,SAi\u22121}andSBj\u2208\u0338{SB1,...,SBi\u22121}, then by definition our greedy algorithm selects the pair (SAj,SBj)so the setSAj,Bjis added in the cov",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 5": {
    "total_score": 0.003179798924479776,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "In every iteration, our greedy algorithm computes a set SA,Bthat covers the most uncovered elements in U\u2032. LetCbe the family of sets returned by the greedy algorithm executed on (U\u2032,S\u2032). Every selected set in Cis a pair of sets inS. Hence,|X|\u22642(lnn+1)|C\u2217|. \u25a1 Notice that there exists O(m1m2)=O(m2)pairs of sets with different colors. At the beginning of the algorithm, for every pair of sets we compute the number of elements they cover in O(m2n) time. Each time we cover a new element we update the counters inO(m2)time. In total, our algorithm runs in O(m2n)time. This algorithm can be extended to kgroups, straightforwardly. In each iteration, we find the ksets of different color that cover the the most uncovered elements. The running time increases to O(mkn). Theorem 1. There exists an(lnn+1)-approximation algorithm for the FSC problem that runs in O(mkn)time.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph 6": {
    "total_score": 0.0010599329748265918,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "While our algorithm returns an O(logn)-approximation, which is the best that someone can hope for the FSC, the running time depends exponentially on the number of groups k. Next, we propose two fasterO(poly(n,m,k))time randomized algorithms with the same asymptotic approximation.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  }
}
